// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row impl_fn_return_stack_env_dangles (tier 1).
// A CLOSURE RETURNED AS `impl Fn` KEEPS ITS CAPTURED ENVIRONMENT IN THE DEAD
// FRAME. The `{fn,env}` PAIR now survives the return; the ENV does not.
//
// ⚠ RE-ARMED 2026-09-09, SAME COMMIT AS THE LANDING THAT RETIRED ITS OLD
// INSTRUMENT — read this before trusting anything the previous header said.
//
// The row was recorded `run 139` against the single-fn spelling now kept below
// as PART 1. That spelling no longer SIGSEGVs: `repr_return_type` moved
// FatClosure into the by-value return row, so `mk` hands back the 16-byte
// {fn,env} pair instead of a pointer to its own frame slot, and PART 1 exits 0
// with the RIGHT answer. Its defect did not move — it still reads
// `k` out of a dead frame, which valgrind reports as two "Conditional jump or
// move depends on uninitialised value(s)" records in `main` and no exit code
// reports at all. A stronger rule upstream retired this row's INSTRUMENT and
// left its DEFECT where it was.
//
// PART 2 is the replacement carrier, and it restores a RUN-VISIBLE oracle:
// two closures built by two different functions and held alive at once. Both
// envs are `create_entry_alloca` slots at the same depth, so the second call's
// frame reuses the first's env and `f()` reads `40` instead of `3`.
//   want   f() + g() == 43, exit 0
//   today  wrong sum, exit 1, plus 2 valgrind uninitialised-read records
//
// ROOT, MEASURED, AND IT IS NOT THE ONE THE OLD HEADER NAMED. The old header
// named the `ec->escapes = true` arm in `SemaChecker`'s closure-literal
// lowering (src/compiler/sema_expr.cpp) as the root. On 2026-09-09 that arm was
// widened to fire for ANY callable hint AND MEASURED ON TOP OF THE PAIR FIX:
// PART 1 read 0 / 2 valgrind records and PART 2 read 1 / 2 valgrind records
// ARMED AND UNARMED, digit for digit. `escapes` is not this row's root. The
// hint does not even reach a `-> impl Fn` return: a returned `|x| ...` with an
// untyped param infers `void`, which is the same hint failing at its other
// consumer.
//
// SO THE REMAINING WORK IS THREE DOORS IN SERIES, NOT ONE:
//   1. the fact that a closure literal is in RETURN POSITION must reach the
//      escape decision at all (today `hint_closure_formal_` does not carry it);
//   2. `gen_closure` must then take the heap-env path (`heap_env = escapes &&
//      !captures.empty()`);
//   3. and something must FREE that heap env. Nothing does today for a closure
//      that is not boxed: measured 2026-09-09g, the crude escape arm leaks
//      `definitely lost: 16 bytes` at the iterator-adapter shape while still
//      exiting 0. Opening doors 1 and 2 without door 3 trades a dangle for a
//      leak, and a leak has no exit code.
//
// CONTROLS CORRECT ON THIS BINARY, bounding where a fix must not reach:
// the same escaping closure through `Box<dyn Fn() -> i64>` (Box::new copies the
// pair AND heap-allocates the env — clean, 7+7=14); a NON-capturing closure
// returned as `impl Fn` (clean since the pair fix — pinned as the pass fixture
// `fatret_closure_impl_fn_pair_survives`); and the same closure not returned
// but called in place.
package impl_fn_return_stack_env_dangles;

// ── PART 1: the row's original spelling. Exits 0 today with the right answer
// and two valgrind uninitialised-read records. Kept so the evidence runs.
fn mk() -> impl Fn() -> i64 {
    let k: i64 = 7i64;
    return move || -> i64 { return k; };
}

// ── PART 2: the replacement carrier. Two envs at the same frame depth.
fn mk_a() -> impl Fn() -> i64 {
    let k: i64 = 3i64;
    return move || -> i64 { return k; };
}
fn mk_b() -> impl Fn() -> i64 {
    let k: i64 = 40i64;
    return move || -> i64 { return k; };
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let h = mk();
    let a: i64 = h();
    let b: i64 = h();
    if a + b != 14i64 { return 2i32; }

    let f = mk_a();
    let g = mk_b();
    if f() + g() != 43i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
